Now I understand the issue. Looking at the error messages, all tests are failing with "Usage:" message on stderr, which means the program is hitting the usage call. The problem is in the option parsing logic.

Looking at line 316-366, when an option starts with `-` but doesn't match any of the expected patterns (like `--with-filename`, `--no-filename`, etc.), it falls through to the `usage()` call at line 362-365.

The issue is that for short options like `-i`, the code checks if `argv[arg_idx][1] == 'r'` first (line 332), but `-i` doesn't start with `r`. Then it checks if `argv[arg_idx][1] == 'H' || argv[arg_idx][1] == 'h'` (line 348), but `-i` doesn't match that either. So it falls through to the usage error.

The fix is to add a case for when `argv[arg_idx][1] == 'i'` or when the option contains `i` in the combined short options parsing.
